      "sentence_id": "sentence_5bd462eb5d3c9ae5a517",
      "document_start": 59,
      "document_end": 78,
      "text": "2.4 Thermodynamics;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_e61303435b4545eb7f1f",
      "document_start": 79,
      "document_end": 111,
      "text": "intervening text may be omitted]",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_585b6fa27ba3678ae402",
      "document_start": 113,
      "document_end": 228,
      "text": "In many instances, we will find that we require a relationship between internal energy or enthalpy and temperature.",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_da2d684a6b66ae4d68ac",
      "document_start": 229,
      "document_end": 374,
      "text": "If we have a gas that is not reacting and intermolecular forces are small enough to ignore, we can consider the gas to be thermally perfect [12].",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_d71076e3ea15ec85eb7c",
      "document_start": 375,
      "document_end": 477,
      "text": "The implications of this are that internal energy and enthalpy are functions of the temperature alone.",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_23e12bd0ed18efced4a3",
      "document_start": 478,
      "document_end": 564,
      "text": "With this model, we can write expressions for internal energy and enthalpy as follows:",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_527eb32c2ec58f321d97",
      "document_start": 566,
      "document_end": 586,
      "text": "[Source paragraph 2;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_62cd24bd9b6acd1eb723",
      "document_start": 587,
      "document_end": 606,
      "text": "2.4 Thermodynamics;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_cf404708746238edfb2b",
      "document_start": 607,
      "document_end": 639,
      "text": "intervening text may be omitted]",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_e79fa87e46e9bd4f4942",
      "document_start": 641,
      "document_end": 715,
      "text": "Normally,  $c_{\\mathrm{p}}$  and  $c_{\\mathrm{v}}$  vary with temperature.",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_cd1736d3e20d5c22e372",
      "document_start": 716,
      "document_end": 891,
      "text": "In many practical cases, this variation is small, and we can further assume that the gas is calorically perfect, which results in the aforementioned equations being written as",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_580e9b631f0f1e03bf1b",
      "document_start": 893,
      "document_end": 913,
      "text": "[Source paragraph 3;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_80a42185136569321ba9",
      "document_start": 914,
      "document_end": 933,
      "text": "2.4 Thermodynamics;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_895e4060e51a36d928d0",
      "document_start": 934,
      "document_end": 966,
      "text": "intervening text may be omitted]",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_89845f66402df4812abf",
      "document_start": 968,
      "document_end": 1021,
      "text": "then we can write the aforementioned relationships as",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_fb4e8b816674fbbbe984",
      "document_start": 1023,
      "document_end": 1043,
      "text": "[Source paragraph 4;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_e11bf611f870bcd69d30",
      "document_start": 1044,
      "document_end": 1063,
      "text": "2.4 Thermodynamics;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_03924ec40c046fcdbd96",
      "document_start": 1064,
      "document_end": 1096,
      "text": "intervening text may be omitted]",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_e4f5b39be0d8febd0fa9",
      "document_start": 1098,
      "document_end": 1173,
      "text": "The second law of thermodynamics defines the concept of entropy for us [1].",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_65676525ce067490066f",
      "document_start": 1174,
      "document_end": 1224,
      "text": "We know from the second law of thermodynamics that",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_db75ce9a337fe8066419",
      "document_start": 1226,
      "document_end": 1246,
      "text": "[Source paragraph 5;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_e502bc894262506f68c9",
      "document_start": 1247,
      "document_end": 1266,
      "text": "2.4 Thermodynamics;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_902807395231ad52ca6a",
      "document_start": 1267,
      "document_end": 1299,
      "text": "intervening text may be omitted]",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_5fcd649f90618b8bb7f6",
      "document_start": 1301,
      "document_end": 1401,
      "text": "If we evaluate Equations 2.57 and 2.58 under the assumptions of a calorically perfect gas, we obtain",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_b4d67bfc462fb880e331",
      "document_start": 1403,
      "document_end": 1423,
      "text": "[Source paragraph 6;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_b766a6b10be682bc2503",
      "document_start": 1424,
      "document_end": 1443,
      "text": "2.4 Thermodynamics;",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_cdba6f74d195a24c204a",
      "document_start": 1444,
      "document_end": 1476,
      "text": "intervening text may be omitted]",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_1a7770887139eacf0345",
      "document_start": 1478,
      "document_end": 1592,
      "text": "In these expressions, the subscripts 1 and 2 indicate the initial and final states of the substance, respectively.",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_aca46f7178a204cb5a17",
      "document_start": 1593,
      "document_end": 1664,
      "text": "An isentropic process is a process in which there is no entropy change.",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_ed756fa43677f82c8111",
      "document_start": 1665,
      "document_end": 1708,
      "text": "This is also known as a reversible process.",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_09fcb7f3cd376713485c",
      "document_start": 1709,
      "document_end": 1783,
      "text": "In a real system, entropy must always increase or, at best, stay constant.",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_062d162387edcab8bdd1",
      "document_start": 1784,
      "document_end": 1867,
      "text": "Many processes have slight enough entropy increases as to be considered isentropic.",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_bb1b032052cdda416ac1",
      "document_start": 1868,
      "document_end": 1959,
      "text": "Isentropic processes are also excellent for examining theoretical limits on real processes.",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_e147fa686f9c63ac56d7",
      "document_start": 1960,
      "document_end": 2083,
      "text": "If we examine Equations 2.59 and 2.60 under an isentropic assumption, we see that the left-hand side (LHS) is zero in both.",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "sentence_e1d91039684ceec808d8",
      "document_start": 2084,
      "document_end": 2156,
      "text": "This has implications that allow us to write (for an isentropic process)",
      "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b"
    }
  ],
