        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sentence_1a7770887139eacf0345",
          "document_start": 1478,
          "document_end": 1592,
          "text": "In these expressions, the subscripts 1 and 2 indicate the initial and final states of the substance, respectively.",
          "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b",
          "start": 1478,
          "end": 1592,
          "chunk_start": 1478,
          "chunk_end": 1592,
          "sentence_document_start": 1478,
          "sentence_document_end": 1592,
          "sentence_complete": true
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sentence_aca46f7178a204cb5a17",
          "document_start": 1593,
          "document_end": 1664,
          "text": "An isentropic process is a process in which there is no entropy change.",
          "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b",
          "start": 1593,
          "end": 1664,
          "chunk_start": 1593,
          "chunk_end": 1664,
          "sentence_document_start": 1593,
          "sentence_document_end": 1664,
          "sentence_complete": true
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sentence_ed756fa43677f82c8111",
          "document_start": 1665,
          "document_end": 1708,
          "text": "This is also known as a reversible process.",
          "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b",
          "start": 1665,
          "end": 1708,
          "chunk_start": 1665,
          "chunk_end": 1708,
          "sentence_document_start": 1665,
          "sentence_document_end": 1708,
          "sentence_complete": true
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sentence_09fcb7f3cd376713485c",
          "document_start": 1709,
          "document_end": 1783,
          "text": "In a real system, entropy must always increase or, at best, stay constant.",
          "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b",
          "start": 1709,
          "end": 1783,
          "chunk_start": 1709,
          "chunk_end": 1783,
          "sentence_document_start": 1709,
          "sentence_document_end": 1783,
          "sentence_complete": true
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sentence_062d162387edcab8bdd1",
          "document_start": 1784,
          "document_end": 1867,
          "text": "Many processes have slight enough entropy increases as to be considered isentropic.",
          "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b",
          "start": 1784,
          "end": 1867,
          "chunk_start": 1784,
          "chunk_end": 1867,
          "sentence_document_start": 1784,
          "sentence_document_end": 1867,
          "sentence_complete": true
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sentence_bb1b032052cdda416ac1",
          "document_start": 1868,
          "document_end": 1959,
          "text": "Isentropic processes are also excellent for examining theoretical limits on real processes.",
          "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b",
          "start": 1868,
          "end": 1959,
          "chunk_start": 1868,
          "chunk_end": 1959,
          "sentence_document_start": 1868,
          "sentence_document_end": 1959,
          "sentence_complete": true
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sentence_e147fa686f9c63ac56d7",
          "document_start": 1960,
          "document_end": 2083,
          "text": "If we examine Equations 2.59 and 2.60 under an isentropic assumption, we see that the left-hand side (LHS) is zero in both.",
          "section_id": "section_f5d59be5457b",
          "start": 1960,
          "end": 2083,
          "chunk_start": 1960,
          "chunk_end": 2083,
          "sentence_document_start": 1960,
          "sentence_document_end": 2083,
          "sentence_complete": true
        },
        {
